messages:
alex: Hey team — running into a weird one on the sample app in our pnpm monorepo when targeting the edge runtime. Works locally in node, but deploy fails with an import error during the edge build.

alex: Error: Cannot find module 'some-sdk' or it's not exported from package (stack: /workspace/.netlify/functions/edge/index.mjs)

alex: package.json on the SDK has an "exports" field and only an "import" entry (ESM-only). We're using a consumer package that expects require. pnpm hoists differently so the resolver picks the ESM entry and edge builder chokes.

marta: ugh yep, exports + pnpm hoisting is a fun combo. Can you paste the SDK package.json snippet? :eyes:

alex: Sure — snippet: 
```json
"exports": {
  ".": {
    "import": "./dist/index.mjs",
    "require": "./dist/index.cjs"
  },
  "./package.json": "./package.json"
}
```

kevin: two angles: 1) edge runtime often expects pure ESM, 2) pnpm hoisting can cause resolution to pick a different bundle at workspace root. Are you bundling for edge?

alex: We're not bundling — relying on the SDK being ESM/CJS dual. Netlify edge builder is doing some static resolution step and failing. Also noticed our auth retry logic behaves oddly on edge — retries spin until timeout and then aborts without clearing the inflight fetch. Might be AbortController incompatibility.

jen: For the pnpm part try adding a public hoist pattern or enable shamefully-hoist in .npmrc temporarily to see if hoist fixes resolution: 
```.npmrc
public-hoist-pattern[]=@redwood-inference/*
shamefully-hoist=true
```

marta: alternatively, make the SDK exports explicit with conditional exports for the edge: 
```json
"exports": {
  "import": {"edge": "./dist/index.edge.mjs", "default": "./dist/index.mjs"},
  "require": "./dist/index.cjs"
}
```

kevin: also try adding a small edge-specific build step: bundle the edge entry with esbuild targeting the edge runtime. That removes resolver ambiguity. Example: 
```
esbuild src/edge-entry.ts --bundle --platform=neutral --format=esm --outfile=dist/index.edge.mjs
```

alex: Good call. Will try bundling. For the auth/retry: anyone seen retries hang on edge? We use fetch + AbortController in sdk/retry.ts — Node native Abort behaves slightly different than the edge polyfill.

jen: Yep, edge runtimes (V8 isolates) often have a different AbortController lifecycle. Make sure retry backoff uses a per-attempt AbortSignal that is cleaned up and that your retry logic doesn't re-use the same controller across attempts. Also avoid calling signal.throwIfAborted in polyfills.

marta: quick diff we shipped to another repo: 
```ts
// retry.ts (edge-safe)
export async function fetchWithRetry(input, init) {
  for (let i=0;i<max;i++) {
    const controller = new AbortController();
    const signal = controller.signal;
    const attemptInit = {...init, signal};
    try {
      const res = await fetch(input, attemptInit);
      if (!res.ok) throw res;
      return res;
    } catch (e) {
      controller.abort();
      if (i===max-1) throw e;
      await delay(backoff(i));
    }
  }
}
```

kevin: also check for node builtins leaked in the bundle like "node:buffer" or "process" — edge will fail if you import node builtins. esbuild flag --platform=browser or --platform=neutral + external might help.

alex: Thanks — I repro'd by bundling with esbuild + adding the edge-specific export. Deploy now passes further than before, and the retry hang looks fixed after isolating AbortController per-attempt. Will follow up with a PR to SDK to add an edge entry and note in the monorepo docs how to configure pnpm hoisting. :thumbsup:

deploy-bot: Build succeeded — edge function deploy (sha: a1b2c3) — duration 2m12s :rocket:

jen: Nice! Can you add a short note to samples/README about "pnpm public-hoist-pattern vs esbuild edge bundle" so onboarding users see the tradeoffs?

alex: Yep will add docs + PR for edge export pattern and retry guardrails. Thanks all.
